\layout Question

The diagram below gives the state diagram for a discrete time 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/discreteMM1State.eps
	width 100text%

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In the system you may take the probability of a birth to be 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(b\right)=\lambda\delta t$
\end_inset 

 and the probability of a death to be 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(d\right)=\mu\delta t$
\end_inset 

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta t$
\end_inset 

 is the change in time for each time step and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mu+\lambda\right)\delta t<1$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Qupart

Derive the steady state distribution for this queue in terms of its utilization
 factor.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{qup:steadyState}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

30
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 

As a birth-death process the stationary distribution of the queue may be
 found using detailed balance.
 We therefore look at the flow across a boundary between two states.
\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/boundaryFlowDiscreteMM1.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

For this flow to balance we must have, at steady state,
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)P\left(d\right)=P\left(k-1\right)P\left(b\right)\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)\mu\delta t=P\left(k-1\right)\lambda\delta t\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)\mu=P\left(k-1\right)\lambda\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

Taking the utilisation factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho^{k}P\left(0\right)\]

\end_inset 

Using the normalisation condition to find 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)=1\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}=1\]

\end_inset 

The infinite series converges for 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho<1$
\end_inset 

 (equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu>\lambda$
\end_inset 

) giving
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}=\frac{1}{1-\rho}\]

\end_inset 

 substituting back in we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=1-\rho\]

\end_inset 

and finally
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho^{k}\left(1-\rho\right)\]

\end_inset 

which is recognised as a geometric distribution.
\layout Standard


\series bold 
\emph on 
5 for the boundary flow diagram 10 for the correct value in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

, 10 for the correct derivation of 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 and 5 for the right answer.
\series default 
\emph default 

\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
}{0}
\end_inset 


\layout Qupart

Derive the mean of this distribution.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

20
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 

The mean has the form 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k\rho^{k}\left(1-\rho\right)\]

\end_inset 

Extracting one 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\left(1-\rho\right)$
\end_inset 

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\rho\left(1-\rho\right)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k\rho^{k-1}\]

\end_inset 

which is recognised as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\rho\left(1-\rho\right)\frac{d}{d\rho}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}\]

\end_inset 

which can be written
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\rho\left(1-\rho\right)\frac{d\left(1-\rho\right)^{-1}}{d\rho}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\rho\left(1-\rho\right)\frac{1}{\left(1-\rho\right)^{2}}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
