answer{
\end_inset 

Typically in queueing problems we are interested in expectations under steady
 state distribution.
 Ideally we want to compute the steady state distribution's average, which
 we can do for the discrete 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 However for more complex queueing systems we may not be able to compute
 this average in which case we try and approximated it with a sample based
 average.
 Typically these approximations involve sampling from a Markov chain.
 Averaging these values directly leads to a time based average, for a sample
 based average we need to samples to be 
\emph on 
independent
\emph default 
.
 To estimate the sample based average accurately we do not take every sample
 from the Markov chain, but we wait between samples.
\layout Standard

Finally if the effective state space is large (the state space that the
 queue is likely to visit) then we may have to wait a long time for enough
 samples to get an accurate idea of the steady state distribution, it is
 also difficult to tell how long we need to wait.
