Explain why the probability distribution for the time interval between the
 start of the transmission of a given packet and the end of the previous
 transmission (the service time distribution) is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X=1+kn\right)=\left(1-p\right)p^{k}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

15
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 

The distribution represents the probability that there are 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 errors followed 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{k}$
\end_inset 

 followed by a successful transmission 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1-p\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 The length of time that this takes will be 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 for each failure (1 for transmission and 
\begin_inset Formula $n-1$
\end_inset 

 waiting for the acknowledgement ) plus the length of transmission for a
 single packet.
 
\series bold 
\emph on 
five for k retransmission probability 5 for successful transmission probability
 and 5 for associated length of time.

\series default 
\emph default 
 
