\layout Question

When simulating networks of queues we have to resort to approximations.
 In this question we will review some of the issues that arise when performing
 these approximations.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Qupart

A small system is modelled as two queues.
 Packets arrive at the first queue as a Poisson stream with exponentially
 distributed packet lengths.
 After leaving the first queue the packets enter a second queue.
 The service time for each packet at both queues is proportional to each
 packet's length.
 Explain how the second queue's size, as perceived by an arriving packet,
 will be related to the arriving packet's length.
 How will this affect the waiting time for the arriving packet at the second
 queue? 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

25
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 

The longer packets will arrive at the second queue to find a shorter queue
 than the shorter packets.
 Or in other words queue length at the second queue are anti correlated
 with packet lengths.
 This is because, while the longer packet was being served at the first
 queue there is time for the second queue to empty out.
 This is analogous to a slow moving truck driver who will generally see
 an open road in front of him.
\layout Standard


