What does Jackson's theorem state and what are its assumptions? What is
 the average number of packets in a system according to Jackson's theorem?
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

25
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 

Jackson's theorem states that if packet interarrival times after passing
 through the first queue are not correlated with their lengths (Kleinrock's
 independence assumption) and if randomisation is used to divert traffic
 along the different routes then the average number of packets in the system
 can be derived as if each queue where 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/$
\end_inset 

1.
 This gives the number of packets in the system as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\sum_{ij}\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\lambda_{ij}-\mu_{ij}}\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{ij}$
\end_inset 

 is the traffic flow rate between node 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{ij}$
\end_inset 

 is the average service time between node 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 and node 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

.
 
